  "info": {
    "title": "Schelling segregation (grid)",
    "category": "Agent-based",
    "description": "Schelling's 1971 tipping-point model: 320 Person agents (160 Red, 160 Blue -- colour IS the state, since only NEIGHBOURS(pop, state) can count a neighbourhood by category) scattered over 80% of a 20x20 torus grid. Each tick, an agent computes the SHARE of its 8 Moore neighbours that share its own colour (like_frac, an ordinary attribute so it shows up in Agent attributes/End Mean) and, if that share is below Tolerance, jumps to a uniformly drawn EMPTY cell (move_random(free=True), Schelling's own move rule -- no other feature needed). With Tolerance in the classic 0.3-0.5 band the population-wide like_frac climbs from its random-mixing baseline (~0.5, two colours) to a segregated plateau around 0.6-0.7 within a few dozen ticks, even though every individual agent is only mildly colour-prejudiced -- the model's own point. The grid and the 320 starting cells are fixed (a fixed shuffle, so the run is exactly reproducible); Tolerance is this instance's own."
  },
